{
 "id": "proof-assistant-repos",
 "title": "New GitHub repositories written for a proof assistant",
 "description": "Public repositories whose main language is Lean, Rocq, Isabelle or Agda, per million new public repositories, over the three months to each date. Lean is the language AI theorem provers mostly write in; the others are there for comparison.",
 "group": "adoption",
 "sources": [
  {
   "name": "GitHub repository search",
   "url": "https://docs.github.com/en/rest/search/search#search-repositories"
  }
 ],
 "y": {
  "title": "Per million new repositories",
  "log": true,
  "tickformat": ",.3~r"
 },
 "mode": "recompute",
 "annotations": true,
 "line_shape": "linear",
 "notes": "Dividing by every new public repository takes out GitHub's own growth: half a million a month in 2016, more than eleven million by late 2026. A repository counts only if most of its code is in the language, as GitHub detects it, and forks are left out. GitHub does not tell Lean 3 from Lean 4, and still calls Rocq by its old name, Coq. Many repositories are coursework or experiments, and a class that gives every student a repository shows up as a hump, as in Lean in early 2020. GitHub gives a repository a language only once code is pushed to it, so the latest months can still rise. Nothing here says who or what wrote the code.",
